!deck: Math::Linear Algebra
!type: Basic Math
!tags: Math Linear-Algebra Matrix

!id: 5Eph1RPRiQ
!front: What is an **eigenvector** \( \mathbf{v} \) of a square matrix \( A \)?
!back:
An **eigenvector** of \( A \) is a *non-zero* vector \( \mathbf{v} \) such that

\[ A \mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v} \]

for some scalar \( \lambda \in \mathbb{R} \).

The scalar \( \lambda \) is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \).
!extra: Unlike \( \mathbf{v} \), the eigenvalue \( \lambda \) may be zero, and — even for a real matrix \( A \) — need not be real.
~~~

!id: jvsGiqOtu3
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of an eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \) of \( A \) and its eigenvalue \( \lambda \).
!back:
The eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \) spans a line through the origin that is mapped to itself — an invariant direction — under the linear map \( A \).

The eigenvalue \( \lambda \) is the factor by which \( A \) stretches, shrinks, or reverses vectors along that line; every other direction is, in general, moved off its own line by \( A \).

![Eigenvector v on an invariant line: Av lands back on that same line, scaled by lambda. A generic vector u is not an eigenvector, and Au leaves the line u started on](img/invariant-direction.svg)
~~~

!id: fP53MzFi6p
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of an eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \) of \( A \) with eigenvalue \( \lambda = 0 \).
!back: \( A \) collapses the entire line spanned by \( \mathbf{v} \) to the origin. That direction lies in the null space of \( A \), so \( A \) is singular.
~~~

!id: hwnW2YYG9U
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of an eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \) of \( A \) with eigenvalue \( 0 < \lambda < 1 \).
!back: \( A \) shrinks vectors along the line spanned by \( \mathbf{v} \) toward the origin, without reversing their direction.
~~~

!id: 1jjKVLij8M
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of an eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \) of \( A \) with eigenvalue \( \lambda = 1 \).
!back: \( A \) fixes every vector along the line spanned by \( \mathbf{v} \) exactly — that direction is left pointwise invariant.
~~~

!id: LBBWgsPq97
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of an eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \) of \( A \) with eigenvalue \( \lambda > 1 \).
!back: \( A \) stretches vectors along the line spanned by \( \mathbf{v} \) further from the origin, without reversing their direction.
~~~

!id: XWcrP8FPNH
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of an eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \) of \( A \) with eigenvalue \( \lambda < 0 \).
!back: \( A \) reverses the direction of vectors along the line spanned by \( \mathbf{v} \), mapping them to the opposite ray, while scaling their length by \( |\lambda| \).
!extra: At \( \lambda = -1 \) this is a pure reflection through the origin along that line, with no change in length.
~~~

!deck: Math (Staging)::Eigenvectors

!id: iaLlj3UXT2
!front: What is the eigenspace \( E_\lambda(A) \) of a square matrix \( A \) for a scalar \( \lambda \)?
!back:
\[ E_\lambda(A) = \{ \mathbf{v} : A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v} \} = \operatorname{Null}(A - \lambda I), \]
the null space of \( A - \lambda I \).

- It is always a subspace, since it is the null space of the linear map \( A - \lambda I \).
- It contains a nonzero vector — an eigenvector — exactly when \( \lambda \) is an eigenvalue of \( A \); otherwise \( E_\lambda(A) = \{\mathbf{0}\} \).
~~~

!id: e9xyGFGY3L
!front: Derive why the eigenvalues of a square matrix \( A \) are exactly the roots of the characteristic equation \( \det(A - \lambda I) = 0 \).
!back:
Let:

- \( A \) be \( n \times n \), \( \lambda \) a scalar, \( \mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0} \).

To prove: \( A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v} \) has a nonzero solution \( \mathbf{v} \) if and only if \( \det(A - \lambda I) = 0 \).

- \( A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v} \) rearranges to \( (A - \lambda I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0} \).
- This homogeneous system has a nonzero solution exactly when \( A - \lambda I \) is singular.
- A square matrix is singular exactly when its determinant is zero, so a nonzero \( \mathbf{v} \) exists exactly when \( \det(A - \lambda I) = 0 \).
!extra: \( \det(A - \lambda I) \) expands to a degree-\( n \) polynomial in \( \lambda \) — the characteristic polynomial of \( A \). By the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra it has exactly \( n \) roots over \( \mathbb{C} \), counted with multiplicity, so an \( n \times n \) matrix has at most \( n \) distinct eigenvalues.
~~~

!id: cWxAqrmTes
!front: What is the algebraic multiplicity of an eigenvalue \( \lambda \) of a square matrix \( A \)?
!back: The multiplicity of \( \lambda \) as a root of the characteristic polynomial \( \det(A - \lambda I) = 0 \) — the largest \( k \) such that \( (x - \lambda)^k \) divides the characteristic polynomial.
~~~

!id: z13CTT4mfg
!front: What is the geometric multiplicity of an eigenvalue \( \lambda \) of a square matrix \( A \)?
!back: The dimension of the eigenspace \( E_\lambda(A) = \operatorname{Null}(A - \lambda I) \) — the maximum number of linearly independent eigenvectors associated with \( \lambda \).
~~~

!id: F7vNNdceoU
!front: How do the algebraic and geometric multiplicities of an eigenvalue \( \lambda \) of \( A \) compare, and why?
!back:
The geometric multiplicity is always at most the algebraic multiplicity:
\[ 1 \le \dim E_\lambda(A) \le m_{\text{alg}}(\lambda). \]
Sketch: if \( \dim E_\lambda(A) = k \), extend a basis of \( E_\lambda(A) \) to a basis of \( \mathbb{R}^n \). In this basis \( A \) is similar to a block matrix with \( \lambda I_k \) in the top-left block, so \( (x-\lambda)^k \) already divides the characteristic polynomial — forcing the algebraic multiplicity to be at least \( k \).
!extra: The inequality can be strict — e.g. for a defective matrix, whose eigenspace is smaller than its algebraic multiplicity suggests.
~~~

!id: HfMEty9hvi
!front: What are the eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix \( A \), and why?
!back:
Its diagonal entries \( a_{11}, \dots, a_{nn} \). For a triangular matrix, \( A - \lambda I \) is also triangular, and the determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries:
\[ \det(A - \lambda I) = \prod_{i=1}^n (a_{ii} - \lambda). \]
This vanishes exactly when \( \lambda = a_{ii} \) for some \( i \).
~~~

!id: omTrtntaqY
!front: How does the determinant of a square matrix \( A \) relate to its eigenvalues?
!back:
\[ \det(A) = \prod_{i=1}^n \lambda_i, \]
the product of the eigenvalues, counted with algebraic multiplicity over \( \mathbb{C} \). This follows by factoring the characteristic polynomial as \( \det(A - \lambda I) = \prod_i (\lambda_i - \lambda) \) and evaluating at \( \lambda = 0 \).
~~~

!id: zfkWHY7lkj
!front: How does the trace of a square matrix \( A \) relate to its eigenvalues?
!back:
\[ \operatorname{tr}(A) = \sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i, \]
the sum of the eigenvalues, counted with algebraic multiplicity over \( \mathbb{C} \). This follows by comparing the coefficient of \( \lambda^{n-1} \) in the characteristic polynomial \( \det(A - \lambda I) \), computed both by cofactor expansion and by expanding the factored form \( \prod_i (\lambda_i - \lambda) \).
!extra: Both the sum and the product are always real, even when individual eigenvalues of a real matrix are complex, since complex eigenvalues of a real matrix occur in conjugate pairs.
~~~

!id: rsf9I2T58L
!front: Why is \( 0 \) an eigenvalue of a square matrix \( A \) if and only if \( A \) is singular?
!back: \( 0 \) is an eigenvalue exactly when \( A\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0} \) for some \( \mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0} \) — exactly the condition for \( A \) to have a nonzero null space, i.e. to be singular.
!extra: Equivalently, via \( \det(A) = \prod_i \lambda_i \): the product is zero exactly when some \( \lambda_i = 0 \).
~~~

!id: OnhmFCsEEj
!front: Does the \( 2 \times 2 \) rotation matrix \( R_\theta = \begin{pmatrix} \cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\ \sin\theta & \cos\theta \end{pmatrix} \) have real eigenvectors when \( \theta \not\equiv 0, \pi \pmod{2\pi} \)? Justify.
!back:
No. Its characteristic polynomial is
\[ \lambda^2 - 2\cos\theta\,\lambda + 1 = 0, \]
with discriminant \( 4\cos^2\theta - 4 = -4\sin^2\theta < 0 \) whenever \( \theta \not\equiv 0, \pi \), so both eigenvalues \( \lambda = \cos\theta \pm i \sin\theta = e^{\pm i\theta} \) are non-real.

Geometrically this must happen: rotating by an angle other than \( 0 \) or \( \pi \) sends every nonzero real vector to a different direction, so no real vector can satisfy \( R_\theta \mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v} \) for real \( \lambda \).

![Three sample vectors v on the unit circle, each with its own line through the origin (dashed); rotating each by theta sends it to R_theta v, visibly off that line](img/rotation-no-eigenvector.svg)
!extra: At \( \theta = 0 \), \( R_\theta = I \), so every vector is an eigenvector with \( \lambda = 1 \); at \( \theta = \pi \), \( R_\theta = -I \), so every vector is an eigenvector with \( \lambda = -1 \).
~~~

!id: HdpffYPEZs
!front: What does it mean for two square matrices \( A \) and \( B \) to be similar?
!back: \( A \) and \( B \) are similar if \( B = P^{-1}AP \) for some invertible matrix \( P \) — equivalently, they represent the same linear map with respect to different bases.
~~~

!id: qghEgOdbd3
!front: Derive why similar matrices \( A \) and \( B = P^{-1}AP \) have the same characteristic polynomial, and hence the same eigenvalues with the same algebraic multiplicities.
!back:
Let:

- \( B = P^{-1}AP \) for some invertible \( P \).

To prove: \( \det(B - \lambda I) = \det(A - \lambda I) \) for every \( \lambda \).

- \( B - \lambda I = P^{-1}AP - \lambda I = P^{-1}(A - \lambda I)P \), since \( \lambda I = P^{-1}(\lambda I) P \).
- Taking determinants and using multiplicativity, \( \det(B-\lambda I) = \det(P^{-1})\det(A-\lambda I)\det(P) = \det(A - \lambda I) \), since \( \det(P^{-1}) = 1/\det(P) \).
!extra: Eigenvectors are not preserved — if \( \mathbf{v} \) is an eigenvector of \( A \), the corresponding eigenvector of \( B \) is \( P^{-1}\mathbf{v} \), not \( \mathbf{v} \) itself.
~~~

!id: fTjlO7oiRA
!front: What does it mean for a square matrix \( A \) to be diagonalizable?
!back: \( A \) is diagonalizable if it is similar to a diagonal matrix, i.e. \( A = PDP^{-1} \) for some invertible \( P \) and diagonal \( D \).
~~~

!id: iVbPGIfm5p
!front: Derive why an \( n \times n \) matrix \( A \) is diagonalizable exactly when \( \mathbb{R}^n \) (or \( \mathbb{C}^n \)) has a basis consisting of eigenvectors of \( A \).
!back:
Let:

- \( P \) be the \( n \times n \) matrix whose columns are vectors \( \mathbf{v}_1, \dots, \mathbf{v}_n \).
- \( D = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_n) \).

To prove: \( AP = PD \) with \( P \) invertible if and only if each \( \mathbf{v}_i \) is an eigenvector of \( A \) with eigenvalue \( \lambda_i \) and \( \{\mathbf{v}_1,\dots,\mathbf{v}_n\} \) is a basis.

- Column-by-column, \( AP = PD \) says \( A\mathbf{v}_i = \lambda_i \mathbf{v}_i \) for each \( i \) — the eigenvector equation.
- \( P \) is invertible exactly when its columns are linearly independent, i.e. form a basis.
- When both hold, \( AP = PD \) rearranges to \( A = PDP^{-1} \), the diagonalization.
~~~

!id: 04jHhSAw2e
!front: Derive why eigenvectors of \( A \) corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent.
!back:
Let:

- \( \mathbf{v}_1, \dots, \mathbf{v}_k \) be eigenvectors of \( A \) with pairwise distinct eigenvalues \( \lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_k \).

To prove: \( \mathbf{v}_1, \dots, \mathbf{v}_k \) are linearly independent.

- Induct on \( k \); the case \( k = 1 \) holds since \( \mathbf{v}_1 \neq \mathbf{0} \).
- Suppose \( \mathbf{v}_1, \dots, \mathbf{v}_{k-1} \) are independent, and \( c_1\mathbf{v}_1 + \dots + c_k\mathbf{v}_k = \mathbf{0} \).
- Applying \( A \) gives \( c_1\lambda_1\mathbf{v}_1 + \dots + c_k\lambda_k\mathbf{v}_k = \mathbf{0} \); subtracting \( \lambda_k \) times the original relation gives \( \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} c_i(\lambda_i - \lambda_k)\mathbf{v}_i = \mathbf{0} \).
- By the induction hypothesis, each \( c_i(\lambda_i - \lambda_k) = 0 \); since \( \lambda_i \neq \lambda_k \) for \( i < k \), each \( c_i = 0 \), and then \( c_k\mathbf{v}_k = \mathbf{0} \) forces \( c_k = 0 \) too.
~~~

!id: fFVonyJ58g
!front: Why does an \( n \times n \) matrix \( A \) with \( n \) distinct eigenvalues have to be diagonalizable?
!back: Each eigenvalue contributes at least one eigenvector, and eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are automatically linearly independent. With \( n \) distinct eigenvalues this gives \( n \) linearly independent eigenvectors — a basis of \( \mathbb{R}^n \) (or \( \mathbb{C}^n \)) — so \( A \) is diagonalizable.
!extra: The converse is false: a matrix can be diagonalizable with repeated eigenvalues, e.g. \( A = I \).
~~~

!id: BY6qnHJ3A4
!front: Under what condition on algebraic and geometric multiplicities is a square matrix \( A \) diagonalizable?
!back: \( A \) is diagonalizable if and only if, for every eigenvalue, the geometric multiplicity equals the algebraic multiplicity — equivalently, the geometric multiplicities sum to \( n \), so the eigenspaces together span \( \mathbb{R}^n \) (or \( \mathbb{C}^n \)).
~~~

!id: n6bTIRDa5K
!front: Construct a \( 2 \times 2 \) matrix that is not diagonalizable, and explain why.
!back:
\[ A = \begin{pmatrix} a & 1 \\ 0 & a \end{pmatrix} \]
(a Jordan block) has characteristic polynomial \( (a - \lambda)^2 \), so \( \lambda = a \) is its only eigenvalue, with algebraic multiplicity \( 2 \). But
\[ A - aI = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \]
has rank \( 1 \), so its null space — the eigenspace — is only \( 1 \)-dimensional, spanned by \( (1,0) \). Since the geometric multiplicity \( 1 \) falls short of the algebraic multiplicity \( 2 \), there is no basis of eigenvectors, so \( A \) is not diagonalizable — it is defective.

![For A = [[1,1],[0,1]] (a=1), the x-axis is the only eigenline, fixed pointwise since lambda=1; a generic vector u off that line is sheared to Au, which does not land back on u's own line](img/defective-shear.svg)
~~~

!id: QD8lbZkAxK
!front: Why does diagonalizing \( A = PDP^{-1} \) make computing \( A^k \) easy?
!back:
\[ A^k = (PDP^{-1})^k = PD^kP^{-1}, \]
since the interior \( P^{-1}P \) factors cancel in each product. Since \( D \) is diagonal, \( D^k = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1^k, \dots, \lambda_n^k) \) requires only raising each eigenvalue to the \( k \)-th power, avoiding \( k-1 \) matrix multiplications.
~~~

!id: LyFGh1qqZP
!front: Derive why every eigenvalue of a real symmetric matrix \( A \) is real.
!back:
Let:

- \( A \) be real and symmetric (\( A^T = A \)), \( \lambda \in \mathbb{C} \) an eigenvalue with eigenvector \( \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{C}^n \), \( \mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0} \).
- \( \mathbf{v}^{*} \) denote the conjugate transpose of \( \mathbf{v} \).

To prove: \( \lambda \in \mathbb{R} \).

- \( \mathbf{v}^{*} A \mathbf{v} \) is a scalar, and \( (\mathbf{v}^{*}A\mathbf{v})^{*} = \mathbf{v}^{*}A^{*}\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}^{*}A\mathbf{v} \), using \( A^{*} = A^T = A \) since \( A \) is real symmetric — so \( \mathbf{v}^{*}A\mathbf{v} \) is real.
- Substituting \( A\mathbf{v} = \lambda\mathbf{v} \) gives \( \mathbf{v}^{*}A\mathbf{v} = \lambda\,\mathbf{v}^{*}\mathbf{v} = \lambda\lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert^2 \).
- Since \( \lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert^2 > 0 \) is real and \( \mathbf{v}^{*}A\mathbf{v} \) is real, \( \lambda = \mathbf{v}^{*}A\mathbf{v} / \lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert^2 \) is real.
~~~

!id: qnccqhxKtS
!front: Derive why eigenvectors of a real symmetric matrix \( A \) corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal.
!back:
Let:

- \( A \) be real symmetric, \( A\mathbf{v}_1 = \lambda_1\mathbf{v}_1 \), \( A\mathbf{v}_2 = \lambda_2\mathbf{v}_2 \), with \( \lambda_1 \neq \lambda_2 \) (necessarily real).

To prove: \( \mathbf{v}_1 \cdot \mathbf{v}_2 = 0 \).

- \( \lambda_1(\mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2) = (A\mathbf{v}_1)\cdot\mathbf{v}_2 = \mathbf{v}_1\cdot(A^T\mathbf{v}_2) = \mathbf{v}_1\cdot(A\mathbf{v}_2) = \lambda_2(\mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2) \), using \( (A\mathbf{x})\cdot\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{x}\cdot(A^T\mathbf{y}) \) and \( A^T = A \).
- So \( (\lambda_1 - \lambda_2)(\mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2) = 0 \), and since \( \lambda_1 \neq \lambda_2 \), \( \mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2 = 0 \).
~~~

!id: gfS7b5XYa8
!front: State the Spectral Theorem for real symmetric matrices, and explain why it guarantees more than just the distinct-eigenvalue orthogonality fact.
!back:
If \( A \) is a real symmetric \( n \times n \) matrix, then:

- every eigenvalue of \( A \) is real,
- \( \mathbb{R}^n \) has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors of \( A \),

so \( A = QDQ^T \) for an orthogonal matrix \( Q \) (columns the orthonormal eigenvectors, \( Q^TQ = I \)) and diagonal \( D \) of the eigenvalues.

This is stronger than distinct-eigenvalue orthogonality alone: it guarantees a full orthonormal eigenbasis even when eigenvalues repeat, by choosing an orthonormal basis within each (possibly higher-dimensional) eigenspace — eigenvectors from different eigenspaces are automatically orthogonal, and a symmetric matrix is guaranteed to have enough eigenvectors overall to span \( \mathbb{R}^n \).
~~~

!id: Mex5lmNU6P
!front: Why is a real symmetric matrix never defective, even when it has repeated eigenvalues?
!back: For a real symmetric matrix, the geometric multiplicity of every eigenvalue always equals its algebraic multiplicity — unlike general matrices, where geometric multiplicity can fall short (e.g. a Jordan block). This equality is exactly what the Spectral Theorem's orthonormal eigenbasis guarantees: enough independent eigenvectors always exist to diagonalize \( A \).
~~~

!id: NnzTCU0EBn
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the orthogonal eigenvectors of a real symmetric matrix \( A \).
!back:
They give a set of mutually perpendicular directions — the principal axes — along which \( A \) acts by pure scaling, with no shearing or rotation mixing the directions together. For the quadratic form \( \mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x} = c \), these axes are the axes of symmetry of the resulting ellipse or ellipsoid (or hyperbola, if some eigenvalues are negative).

![The unit circle mapped by the symmetric matrix A = [[2,1],[1,2]] to an ellipse whose semi-axes lie along A's orthogonal eigenvectors (1,1) and (1,-1), with lengths equal to the eigenvalues 3 and 1](img/eigen-ellipse.svg)
~~~
